<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chichimeca</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chichimecs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chichimeca was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> name for the nomadic and semi-nomadic peoples of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and also for migrants from the north who settled in central Mexico. Several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a>, claimed Chichimec ancestry. In the 16th century the Chichimeca resisted Spanish expansion in the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca-war">Chichimeca War</a> of about 1550-1590.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Chichimeca is the name that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> generically applied to nomadic and semi-nomadic peoples established in the present-day Bajío region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> name Chīchīmēcah means inhabitants of Chichiman, a word meaning area of milk. It is sometimes said to be related to chichi, dog, but the vowel lengths differ, and vowel length changes the meaning in Nahuatl. The name carried the same meaning as the Roman term barbarian, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> adopted it in that pejorative sense.</p><p>To the Spanish the Chichimeca were wild people who had no fixed dwelling places and lived by hunting. In fact it was a broad term used by outsiders, Spanish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> speakers alike, for many different peoples whose cultures ranged from hunter-gatherers to sedentary agriculturalists with sophisticated political organisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the late 16th century Gonzalo de las Casas, who had received an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> near Durango and fought in the wars against the Chichimec peoples, wrote the Report of the Chichimeca and the Justness of the War Against Them. He named the Pame, Guachichil, Guamare and Zacateco, who lived in the area then known as La Gran Chichimeca, and wrote that they covered only their genitals, painted their bodies and ate only game, roots and berries. To the Spanish they were wild people north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> who fiercely resisted intrusion into land that contained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines.</p><p>In the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca-war">Chichimeca War</a>, from 1550 to 1590, Chichimeca raids, ambushes of critical economic routes and pillaging proved effective. The Spanish at first tried to defeat them in a war of fire and blood, but eventually sought peace, since they could not win. To end the war they adopted a Purchase for Peace programme, providing food, tools, livestock and land, sending Spanish to teach agriculture and converting the Chichimeca to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Within a century the Spanish and Chichimeca had assimilated. The interaction brought a drastic fall in the population of all the peoples known collectively as Chichimecas, and their eventual disappearance as peoples, except for the Pames of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> and the related Chichimeca-Jonaz of the Sierra Gorda in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a>. Today only one ethnic group is customarily called Chichimec, the Chichimeca Jonaz, a few thousand of whom live in the state of Guanajuato.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chichimeca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Colonial chronicles say that the Toltecs combined Tolteca-<b>Chichimeca</b> migrants from the north with the Nonoalca, a people associated with the Gulf Coast, and they credit the city’s rise to leaders such as Mixcoatl and his son Ce Acatl Topiltzin Quetzalcoatl, figures of legend rather than of history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of Tula, groups of Nahuatl speakers, known as <b>Chichimecs</b> and later Aztecs, migrated into the Valley of Mexico, among them the Mexica, who according to their tradition came from a place called Aztlan and founded their city, Tenochtitlan, on an island in Lake Texcoco in…</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Traditions in the Historia Tolteca-Chichimeca say Tolteca-<b>Chichimeca</b> groups took control of the city in 1168, founding the Postclassic state.</blockquote><a href="/state/cholula">Cholula</a></li><li><blockquote>Huexotzinco (modern Huejotzingo) was settled by <b>Chichimec</b> and Nahua groups in the Puebla valley in the 12th or 13th century, and grew into a powerful city-state that at times dominated neighbours such as Tlaxcala and Cholula.</blockquote><a href="/state/huexotzinco">Huexotzinco</a></li></ul></section>
